## Why This Is Asked

Temporal-like constraints on feature flags across release cycles test three-valued propagation and branching heuristics—skills essential for configuration validation at scale.

## Key Concepts

- Kleene three-valued logic with implication defined as ¬A ∨ B
- Propagation rules for ¬, ∨, ∧, and →
- Backtracking search treating U as a first-class domain value

## Code Example

```javascript
// Three-valued backtracking propagator sketch
const VALUES = { T: 2, U: 1, F: 0 };
const NOT = { T: 'F', F: 'T', U: 'U' };
const OR = (a, b) => VALUES[a] >= VALUES[b] ? a : b;
const AND = (a, b) => VALUES[a] <= VALUES[b] ? a : b;
const IMPLIES = (a, b) => OR(NOT[a], b);

function propagate(constraints, assignment) {
  let changed = true;
  while (changed) {
    changed = false;
    for (const [ante, conseq] of constraints) {
      const a = assignment[ante], c = assignment[conseq];
      if (a === 'F' && c !== 'F') { assignment[conseq] = 'F'; changed = true; }
      if (a === 'T' && c !== 'T') { assignment[conseq] = 'T'; changed = true; }
      if (c === 'F' && a !== 'F') { assignment[ante] = 'F'; changed = true; }
    }
  }
  return assignment;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How does the branching heuristic affect worst-case complexity?
- How would you extend this to N time steps?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason with Kleene semantics and construct a concrete witness with an exact number of U values, a common formal verification skill.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene 3-valued semantics and truth tables
- Boolean connectives as min/max and a→b = ¬a ∨ b
- Exact-U witness construction and targeted propagation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Kleene evaluation helpers
const V = {F:0, U:1, T:2};
function not(x){ return x===V.T?V.F: x===V.F?V.T: V.U; }
function or(x,y){ return Math.max(x,y); }
function and(x,y){ return Math.min(x,y); }
function imp(a,b){ return or(not(a), b); }

const A=V.U, B=V.F, C=V.U, D=V.T;
const c1 = imp(or(A, not(B)), and(C,D));
const c2 = or(not(C), and(A,B));
const c3 = imp(or(D, not(A)), not(B));
console.log([c1,c2,c3].map(v=> v===V.T?'T': v===V.U?'U':'F').join(' '));
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to 5 variables with a mix of constraints and a required total of three U's?
- What are the limitations of using min/max with U in modeling real circuit indeterminacy?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests design of tri-valued constraint systems with a custom operator and backtracking propagation, ensuring candidates reason about Kleene semantics and propagation rules.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene three-valued logic with T > U > F; ∧/∨ act as min/max.
- Custom operator Nand defined as not(and(x,y)).
- Backtracking propagation to prune domains and find a satisfying assignment.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Basic tri-valued lattice: T > U > F
const rank = { 'F':0, 'U':1, 'T':2 };
function not(x){ return x==='T'?'F': x==='F'?'T':'U'; }
function and(a,b){ return rank[a] <= rank[b] ? a : b; }
function or(a,b){ return rank[a] >= rank[b] ? a : b; }
function nand(a,b){ return not(and(a,b)); }
function implies(p,q){ return or(not(p), q); }

// sample assignment
let F1='T', F2='F', F3='U', F4='F';
console.log(implies(or(F1,F2), F3)); // U
console.log(and(F2,F4)); // F
console.log(nand(F3,F4)); // T
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you generalize to N flags and arbitrary constraint sets?
- What changes when moving to a 4-valued logic or alternative lattices?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about constraints in practical feature-flag settings and to design a solver using propagation and backtracking rather than pure search. It also exposes how to craft a constructive counterexample when needed.

## Key Concepts

- Constraint satisfaction with implications and disjunctions
- Unit propagation, backtracking, and a decision trail
- Real-world constraints in deployment feature flags and release gates

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal backtracker for boolean flags with given constraints
function sat(S){
  return (!S.S1 || S.S2) && (!S.S2 || S.S3) && (!S.S4 || !S.S1) && (S.S1 || S.S4) && (S.S2 || S.S4);
}
function satPartial(part){
  const v = (k)=> part.hasOwnProperty(k)? part[k] : null;
  if (v('S1')===true && v('S2')===false) return false;
  if (v('S2')===true && v('S3')===false) return false;
  if (v('S4')===true && v('S1')===true) return false;
  if (v('S1')===false && v('S4')===false) return false;
  if (v('S2')===false && v('S4')===false) return false;
  return true;
}
function solve(partial={}, idx=0){
  const vars=['S1','S2','S3','S4'];
  if (!satPartial(partial)) return null;
  if (idx===vars.length) return partial;
  const name = vars[idx];
  for (const val of [false,true]){
    const next = Object.assign({}, partial, {[name]: val});
    const res = solve(next, idx+1);
    if (res) return res;
  }
  return null;
}
console.log(solve());
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale this to thousands of flags with mostly Horn clauses?
- What optimizations would you add for large constraint sets?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason through 3-valued logic and implement a constrained solver with backtracking—a common pattern in policy/rule engines.

## Key Concepts

- Kleene 3-valued logic with unknown (U) support
- Implication defined as ¬A ∨ B
- Backtracking propagation to avoid false outcomes

## Code Example

```javascript
function kleeneImpl(a,b){
  const not = v => v==='T'?'F': v==='F'?'T':'U';
  const order = {'F':0,'U':1,'T':2};
  const na = not(a);
  const vals = [na, b];
  return vals.reduce((m,x)=> order[x] > order[m] ? x : m, 'F');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize the backtracking algorithm for larger sensor networks?
- Can you extend this approach to handle additional logical operators?
- What strategies would you use to minimize the number of U values in the final assignment?
